## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Test(s) | Effort | Why first |
|----------|-------|---------|--------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` | 5 min | **False-pass risk** — this test provides zero safety |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` | 10 min | Hidden coverage gap — one branch is never tested |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` | 10 min | Flakiness source + adds 2s to every run |
| 4 | Mystery Guest + Resource Optimism | `ImportOrders_FromCsv_ParsesCorrectly` | 10 min | Blocks CI — hard-coded `C:\` path |
| 5 | Exception Handling | `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` | 5 min | Quick win — direct MSTest API replacement |
| 6 | Magic Number + Assertion Roulette | `CalculateTotal_ReturnsCorrectAmount` | 10 min | Readability — hard to diagnose failures |
| 7 | Sensitive Equality | `GetOrderSummary_ReturnsFormattedString` | 10 min | Brittle to formatting changes |
| 8 | Eager Test | `OrderProcessor_FullWorkflow_Succeeds` | 15 min | Split or re-tag as integration |
| 9 | General Fixture | Class-level `_logger` | 2 min | Trivial cleanup |

**Bottom line:** 8 of 9 tests carry at least one cataloged smell. The single clean test (`ValidateOrder_NullOrder_ThrowsArgumentNullException`) demonstrates the team knows how to write good tests — the rest of the suite just needs the same discipline applied consistently. Priorities 1–4 address false-pass and flakiness risks and should be fixed first.
